  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{1-x}>4,\\,x>2.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x>2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >2 \\\\ -x & <-2 \\\\ 1-x & <-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1-x$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>2).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $1-x,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & >4 \\\\ (1-x)\\cdot \\frac{3x+2}{1-x} & <(1-x)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 3x+2 & <4-4x \\\\ 7x & <2 \\\\ x & <\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there are no numbers that satisfy both $x>2$ and $x<\\frac{2}{7}.$ So, the inequality has no solution.",
